# 3장 (5판: 3.42) 영어판: 퀘스트 ID -> (제목, 받을 때, 진행 중, 끝낼 때)
EN = {
"story_3_01": ("The Frozen Net",
 "The Elder sent you? About the ice, then. It's true. Since this winter began it comes a hand's width a day toward the village. After fifteen years of sitting still on the horizon. The northern sea froze in a single night fifteen years ago. My son Elric was aboard Captain Cornelia's ship that day. Neither ship nor crew came home. Sit. It isn't a long story, but it isn't one to hear standing.",
 "Hear out Oscar the fisherman.",
 "You'll come along. …Thank you. I know the waterways. Before it froze I knew that sea like my own palm. I don't know why it froze. For fifteen years that's what I most wanted to know, and least wanted to know. Let's not hurry. The ice swallows the hurried first. We start with the catch. Fish are the first to know how the sea is doing."),
"story_3_02": ("The Fish That Left",
 "Cut a hole at the edge of the ice and drop a line. See what comes up these days. Cod and salmon will do. It used to be an hour's work for a full basket. See how long it takes you. That is the answer.",
 "Catch 4 raw cod and 2 raw salmon and bring them to Oscar.",
 "Took a while, didn't it. And look at these eyes. Clouded. These live deep. With no shallow-water fish left, the deep ones have come up. Means something has taken the shallows for itself. There's something big under the ice. Shut in for fifteen years. …That's for later. The sun's going. Time to light the lantern. Will you come? There's something I want to show you."),
"story_3_03": ("The Lantern Keeper",
 "There's a lamp standing out on the ice. Mine. I've lit it every night for fifteen years. Fresh wick every third day, and on windy nights I relit it three times. Tonight you change it. One lump of coal will do. Listen while you work. I've told this to no one.",
 "Change the wick of the lantern on the ice with 1 coal, and hear Oscar's story.",
 "Well changed. The flame's standing straight. (Oscar looks at the lantern a long time.) You light it for the one coming home to see. But light it for fifteen years and you learn. It isn't for the one coming home. It's so the one who lights it can hold out. Tomorrow morning, go and meet the villagers. Hear about that night before we set out. Go without knowing and what you see on the ice won't mean anything to you."),
"story_3_04": ("The Village That Night",
 "Mr. Oscar sent you? There is a record of that night. A town clerk is bound to take down what people say on the day of a great event. I took down that day's too, fifteen years ago. But I only wrote. Others saw. Hear it from three of them. The smith, the cook, and the guide. When you've heard them all, come back and I will check it against my record.",
 "Hear about that night from Bram the smith, Lorenzo the cook and Morris the guide, then return to Haru the town clerk.",
 "The three accounts agree with my record. I will add one thing. That night fishing boats put out from the dock to save the captain's ship. Not one came back. Mr. Oscar was on the dock that night, and that is all he himself remembers. The village decided not to correct that memory. You will see why for yourself out on the ice. Please do not ask him until he raises it first."),
"story_3_05": ("Getting Ready for the Ice",
 "Walk on the ice, you say. In those shoes? Three days and you lose your toes. I'll make you overshoes with iron studs. Oscar's too. He's worn the same pair fifteen years. Won't let me make new ones. They're what he had on the day his boy left. So his get new soles and nothing else. Bring me iron and leather.",
 "Bring 6 iron ingots and 4 leather to Bram the smith.",
 "Done. Try them. Heel down first. Put your toe down first on ice and you slide. I've resoled Oscar's as well. Don't tell him. If he thinks I swapped them for new he won't wear them. And a favour for me. There was an Order outpost at the mouth of the ice maze. I hung the hinges on that door. Six were on watch that night. One was my apprentice. If you get there, just see whether the door was open or shut."),
"story_3_06": ("The Table Still Set",
 "The outpost at the mouth of the ice maze. Six were on watch the night the sea froze. Not one got out. The Elder said it the day you arrived: one day you'd see what happens when a guardian is touched without a Gate. This is it. I don't know ice. Oscar does. Go with him. There'll be no fighting. Look, and bring back what you see.",
 "Find the outpost in the ice maze and examine the inside with Oscar.",
 "Spoons still in their hands. No time even to run. The Elder called that 'backlash'. What everything around takes when something bursts all at once. They didn't die fighting. They froze at supper. …The door was open, you say. Tell Bram so. The watch left it open. Because they saw someone walking in."),
"story_3_07": ("Six Bowls",
 "The recipe on the back of the roster. 'For those who come in off the ice.' The outpost crew were cooking soup for the sailors walking out. That soup never reached anyone. I'll make it. Six bowls. They say that deep in the maze your lungs freeze from breathing. Eat it and go. As far as where those who were walking out stopped.",
 "Bring 6 spinefish and 4 bowls of mushroom stew to Isabel the archivist.",
 "There. Eat while it's hot. As for the taste… don't tell Lorenzo. But listen. The roster said 'many lights'. The sailors were walking with lamps. Somewhere in the maze is where they stopped. A sailor always writes something where he stops. Oscar told me so. Please find them in order. The first written, the next, the last."),
"story_3_08": ("Thirty, Eighteen, Seven",
 "Deeper in the maze is the shelter where the crew hid. The ones who left the ship and were walking out over the ice. Tortured souls drift there. They are that crew. If they come at you, put them to rest. Don't hate them. They are still walking out. Read the wall in order. From what was written first.",
 "In the shelter in the ice maze, read the writing on the wall in order, and put to rest 3 tortured souls in your way.",
 "Thirty went ashore, then eighteen, then seven. The seven got that far. And to the end they did not blame the captain. 'The scholar in the grey cloak' sold the route. That cloak again. Two name tags turned up. Take them to Oscar. He's the one who must read them. Whether one is Elric's, I didn't look. That is not mine to see first."),
"story_3_09": ("Say Their Names",
 "Name tags, you say. …Give them here. No, wait. Let me breathe. All right. Hand them over. Elric's or not, a sailor's name tag has to be read out loud. That's how the man hears his own name.",
 "Hand the 2 crew name tags to Oscar.",
 "Hassan. And Mare. I know both names. Hassan mended nets well and Mare couldn't sing. (Oscar calls both names out loud toward the ice.) …Not Elric. I can't tell whether that's a mercy. As long as no tag turns up I can keep lighting the lantern. Ask the archivist girl to write them down. And what was on the wall. That boats came out from the village. …Where was I that night? No, don't answer. Not yet."),
"story_3_10": ("Lanterns of the Deep",
 "It's pitch dark under the maze. My lantern won't do. Catch anglerfish. The lure on their heads is the only light that won't go out under the ice. Bring me one lure. And see which way they're holding their lures. A fish doesn't look one way for no reason.",
 "Catch 2 anglerfish and bring 1 esca to Oscar.",
 "This light will do. But they all held their lures toward the middle of the maze, you say. …Bait. The big one uses the little ones' lights as bait. To eat whatever comes to the light. The sailors walked with lamps. They'd have gathered toward each other's lights. And the thing in the middle would have seen those lights too."),
"story_3_11": ("Cracking Ice",
 "Oscar has gone into the maze alone. A voice came out of a crack in the ice. 'Father,' it said. Elric's voice, he says. I heard it too. It sounded real. I did the same, remember. In that deep city where the Warden was. I followed my mother's voice down and… it wasn't my mother. Please hurry. He needs someone to tell him that.",
 "Put to rest the 3 tortured souls holding Oscar in the ice maze.",
 "(Oscar is sitting slumped on the ice.) …I know. Once I got close I knew. Elric never called me 'Father'. He called me 'old man'. Since he was twelve. That was something else wearing his voice. The big one below takes out the voices it swallowed and uses them. …Which means he's in there. The archivist girl said she'd been fooled too. We wanted to be fooled. Both of us."),
"story_3_12": ("The Mother of the Maze",
 "The Mother of the Maze. A deep-sea fish shut in the ice and starved, they say. She uses the voices of the souls she's swallowed as bait. Elric's voice came out of her belly too. Oscar knows it. He says he's coming as far as the door anyway. Take him that far and no further. She is bound in the same ice as what the captain's anchor caught. Put her to rest inside the Gate. She is only starving. She still has to be put to rest.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put the Mother of the Maze to rest.",
 "When she fell she gave up every voice. Dozens of them saying their own names at once. And among them, one. 'Elric. Oscar's son.' Just as the captain used to call him. A name tag came out. And a frozen key. The ghost ship's. …I'll tell Oscar myself. That's for someone who has done his own waiting."),
"story_3_13": ("One Stone",
 "(Oscar is holding the name tag on his open palm. He seems to have been doing so a long time.) Captain Roran told me all of it. He's clumsy with words. That's why I believed him. …There's a place on the ice where I set a stone. Beside the lantern. Not a grave. A marker. Would you hang this there. My hands won't do it.",
 "Hang Elric's name tag on the stone marker on the ice.",
 "It's hung? …Thank you. (Oscar sits down before the stone.) That he was eaten, I understand. But there's something strange. His voice was still there. For fifteen years. Inside a fish, and not rotted away. A dead man's voice doesn't last like that. The crew didn't die. They weren't able to. By that ship's law. …I have to meet the captain. The way to the ship lies through the graveyard of ships."),
"story_3_14": ("The Graveyard of Ships",
 "Past the maze lies the graveyard of ships. The fishing boats that put out from the village that night to save the captain's ship are caught there. Sharks and great eels circle between the hulls. Clear a way. And count the prows. The clerk wouldn't give me the number. He means us to count for ourselves, I suppose.",
 "Defeat 3 maws and 1 eel in the graveyard of ships, and examine the prows.",
 "Twenty, you say. Nineteen with names and one without. Every prow pointing north. Not boats that were running. Boats that were going. …And a scarf in the bottom of the nameless one. A long one, to wrap twice. (Roran says nothing for a long time.) Go to Oscar. Don't ask. Only tell him what you saw. He'll do the rest. I know that face. I saw it in the mirror for twenty years."),
"story_3_15": ("The Twentieth Boat",
 "A boat with no name. A scarf in the bottom. …Sit. It just came back to me. What hasn't in fifteen years. What the clerk never told me. What the village never told me. I'll say it. With my own mouth.",
 "Hear out Oscar the fisherman.",
 "That's all of it. I went out. Twentieth. Last. And I came back alone. On foot. Leaving the boat. The village let it be that I'd stayed on the dock. A man who never went hurts less than one who went and turned round. …The lantern I lit for fifteen years wasn't only for him. I was marking the spot where I turned back. So I wouldn't turn back the next time. Come. This time I go to the end."),
"story_3_16": ("The Morning Star",
 "I wrote down what Oscar said. What did I write? 'He went out twentieth, and came back and lit a lantern.' That's all. I didn't write that he ran. I wrote only what was seen. And the writing on the wall. 'The captain meant to win back the Morning Star.' I've found what the Morning Star is. A wreck report from thirty years ago. When Captain Cornelia was twenty-two. Please read it with me.",
 "Read the thirty-year-old wreck report with Isabel the archivist.",
 "She lost her first ship and thirty-two crew to something huge from under the sea. Only she lived. After that she kept one line her whole life. No one dies on my ship. The grey cloak knew that when he went to her. He said there was 'a power that brings back what is forgotten'. That she could have her thirty-two back. He knew exactly where it hurt most, and named his price there. …But 'it did not break the ship, it carried it' troubles me. Oscar said it the day you first met him. Something big is shut under the ice."),
"story_3_17": ("The Ship That Broke Ice",
 "There's a newer ship caught at the north end of the graveyard. Not fifteen years old. Last autumn's. Pirates. Iron on the prow, breaking ice as they came. They'd heard there was treasure on the ghost ship. I want to see why that ship stopped. A hull caught in ice is squeezed from the sides. But from a distance that one is humped in the middle. Pushed from below.",
 "Find the pirate ship trapped in the ice, examine the deck with Oscar, and defeat 2 remaining pirates.",
 "Not broken. Embraced. As if weighing it. And only the mast carried off. The men not even touched. …The wreck report from thirty years ago said the same. It's that one. The one that took the captain's first ship. Shut in when the sea froze, and starved for fifteen years. Starved of ships. The old fishermen have a name for it. The one that cradles ships. Nerakyss."),
"story_3_18": ("The Nest the Captain Drew",
 "The drawing has opened out. The one the captain drew for ten days. Nerakyss isn't in the Chronicle. It's no guardian of a seal. Only a sea beast that has lived a very long time. But look at this drawing and I think you can see why it takes ships. Please look with me. Tell me if it matches what you saw on the pirate ship.",
 "Examine Cornelia's drawings with Isabel the archivist.",
 "It's a nest. Built of ships. It cradles ships the way a bird sits on eggs. There's no malice in it. It's simply what it does. But at the very heart of that nest is the Morning Star. The captain drew it so. Down to the star on the prow. With the thirty-two still aboard. …I think we must do this before we go to the captain. We can't go to her empty-handed. We can't only say 'let go'. What she couldn't let go of for thirty years, we have to bring ashore first."),
"story_3_19": ("The Evening Star",
 "It doesn't chase people. It chases ships. So we call it up with a ship. The channel the pirates broke hasn't frozen over yet. We'll float an empty boat there. When it rises to cradle it, that's the one time it shows itself above water. Get me an oak boat. And raw cod to line the bottom. It likes a boat a fisherman has sailed. Chooses by smell. …My own boat is in that graveyard, true. But it hasn't even a name.",
 "Bring 6 raw cod to Oscar, and launch an oak boat on the channel by the pirate ship.",
 "She's afloat. Named, too. A sailor doesn't launch a boat without a name. I did it once, fifteen years ago, and saw what came of it. She's the Evening Star. Because she goes to fetch the Morning Star. (Oscar stands at the edge of the ice watching the boat.) When it comes up, I throw the harpoon. This time I don't let go of the oar."),
"story_3_20": ("The One That Cradles Ships",
 "The Evening Star has reached the end of the channel. It will come up. The archivist says Nerakyss isn't bound to a seal, so it can't be called inside a Gate. You fight it as it is, beside the pirate ship. When its arms wrap the boat, that's your opening. Strike the arms first. It isn't angry. After fifteen years of hunger it has a ship at last and thinks we're taking it. It still has to be put to rest. The captain couldn't do it in thirty years.",
 "Put Nerakyss the kraken to rest beside the pirate ship trapped in the ice. (Deal at least 10% of its max health)",
 "As it sank it opened its arms. Let go of everything it held. The ice split and ships came up. Thirty years old, a hundred years old. And one with a star on the prow. The Morning Star. Not even rotted. …The thirty-two were still on deck. Just as the captain drew them. Down to the way each one stood. Their names have to be called. From the list Elena took down. Let's bring them to the village. After thirty years."),
"story_3_21": ("Ashore After Thirty Years",
 "The thirty-two of the Morning Star have been brought to the dock. The list Elena the archivist took down thirty years ago is at the town hall. I will call them one by one. A flower laid for each name is how this village buries its sailors. You need not lay them all. Four will do. Bow, stern, port, starboard. One for each corner of the ship.",
 "Bring 4 flowers, lay them at the dock, and hear the thirty-two names.",
 "Thirty-two, all called. Not one missing. At the foot of the list is the captain's signature. 'Captain Cornelia. Survivors: 1.' Today I struck that number out. I made a column that is neither survived nor died and wrote in it. 'Returned to port: 32.' Thirty years a clerk and I have never made such a column before. Please tell the captain. They have come home."),
"story_3_22": ("What the Dock Remembers",
 "People at the burial said so. The dock was this full the day the captain's ship last sailed. The second floor of the Spiral Abyss is the dock on that day. The Abyss shows the memories of those who passed through. Fifteen years ago the captain took her crew through it before sailing. Please see who saw that ship off. Haru's record has no list of well-wishers. Only for that day.",
 "Pass through the second floor of the Spiral Abyss.",
 "A dock, you say. The captain reading her law to the crew, three hundred hands raised, the village clapping. And in the back row someone in a cloak. Not clapping. Hands behind his back. He was there. He watched the ship leave. He saw three hundred people waving and sent them anyway. …I don't think Haru failed to keep the list of well-wishers. Someone took that one page."),
"story_3_23": ("The Bell Under the Sea",
 "It was on the wall. 'The anchor struck something below. A bell rang. Under the sea.' I know that bell. Every fisherman does. In the middle of the northern sea, where the water is deepest, on calm days a bell would sound from below. Since my grandfather's time. We called it 'the king calling us to supper'. An old tale. Will you hear it? And go to the clerk as well. The town hall has a thousand years of harbour tax ledgers. The first volume is from that king's time.",
 "Hear the tale of the king under the sea from Oscar the fisherman, and ask Haru the town clerk to show you the old ledger.",
 "So that king is in the first volume. Not one line of tax collected, only lines of things given. That would be right. Just as the old tale has it. And the last line was 'the stones of the seawall, to the seven kings'. …And that winter the citadel sank. That's what the anchor struck. The bell of the sunken citadel. The captain's ship dropped anchor on top of it. On the roof of a king who gives whatever he's asked."),
"story_3_24": ("Getting Ready to Dive",
 "Down under the sea, you say. Under the ice. Mad. Fine, mad work needs good preparation. That brass helmet you picked up in the graveyard of ships. It's from the Order's expedition twenty years ago. My father made it. The rubber's all perished. I'll rebind the seams in copper. And kelp. The glass has to be fed kelp oil or it freezes.",
 "Bring 4 copper ingots and 8 kelp to Bram the smith.",
 "Done. Watertight. And this is something Roran left with me. The expedition fished it out of the northern sea twenty years ago, he says. An eye cut from stone. Red on one side, blue on the other. Throw it into the sea and it goes one way only. They didn't know what it was and left it in the store. Roran said, 'I think I know what it is now, so give it to the expedition.' He could have handed it over himself, but he always sends me. He's shy of being thanked."),
"story_3_25": ("The Sunken Citadel",
 "Let's go where the stone eye points. Deep sea. I'm coming. Before the freeze I passed over it hundreds of times. Never once saw what lay below. When you're in, look in order. The door, the wall, then before the throne. If the old tale is right, something will be hanging on the door. Something that came down fifteen years ago.",
 "Find the sunken citadel in the deep ocean and examine the door, the wall, and the space before the throne in order, with Oscar.",
 "An anchor on the door ring. The captain's ship's anchor. That anchor rang the bell, and the bell woke the king. And the captain's request is carved on the wall. 'Let no one die on my ship.' …The king granted it. That's the ice. It was the only way that king had to keep people from dying. To stop everything. It wasn't the captain who froze the sea. It was the one who granted the captain's request. Meaning well. That's the most frightening part."),
"story_3_26": ("Requests at Odds",
 "Oscar told me. The king granted the captain's request and so the sea froze. Then there is one thing for us to do. We ask too. For the ice to be taken back. That king can't refuse, they say. The trouble is that the two requests are at odds. The captain's 'let no one die', and ours 'take it back'. It was written on the floor. When requests are at odds, they are settled by a duel. Sit. We plan how to fight it. The archivist has read the rest of what is written before the throne.",
 "Plan the duel with Captain Roran and Isabel the archivist.",
 "To sum up. You fire only the cannon of the side you're weak to. Weak to fire, the fire cannon. Weak to ice, the ice cannon. When the king leaps to the wall and fires his own, that's when ours can be used again. And do not leave the arena. Not one step. That king can't abide a turned back. …The captain isn't there. So the king will stand in her place. We aren't duelling the captain. We're duelling the captain's request. Don't forget that. Because once we win we have to look her in the face."),
"story_3_27": ("Two Braziers",
 "A request isn't made in words, it seems. It's laid on the braziers. Magma cream on the red one, packed ice on the blue. Looking at the old petitions on the wall, everyone laid on the hottest and the coldest thing they owned. Say your request as you lay it and it's carved into the wall. We've settled what to ask. Captain Roran cut it down to one line. Please lay them on. I'll give you the words.",
 "At the sunken citadel, lay 2 magma cream on the red brazier and 2 packed ice on the blue brazier.",
 "It's carved? You saw? One more line at the end of the wall. 'The Storm Expedition. Request: take back the gift. Let the bound go ashore.' And the answer. Not 'Granted', is it. An answer no one has seen before. 'Another asked first. It shall be settled by a duel. As that one is not here, I take up that one's part.' …The king is rising. He's fighting for the captain. To keep what he gave her."),
"story_3_28": ("The Giving King",
 "The king has risen. Malkuth. The king who gave everything asked of him and sank. He is no guardian of a seal. He can't be called inside a Gate. We duel in that citadel, by that law. Go close to the summoning place before the throne and you'll see what is written about the king. Read, then begin. Only the cannon of your weak side. Do not leave the arena. The whole expedition stands at the door. We'll watch your back. Go.",
 "Duel Malkuth, the Giving King, in the sunken citadel and put him to rest. (Start at the summoning place before the throne. Everyone who fights there is credited.)",
 "The king knelt. He laid his two swords on the floor. The red and the blue. And he said, 'The duel was fair. The request is granted.' Then he put his hand into the blue brazier and put the fire out. With his own hand. …There was a sound from above. Ice splitting. The whole sea. The king said one more thing at the last. 'Tell the captain. It is the first time I have taken back what I gave. Tell her I am sorry.' Let's go and tell her. When it's light. Tonight everyone turns in early. The anchor is still on the door ring."),
"story_3_29": ("The Sound of Thaw",
 "Hear that? The ice crying. First time in fifteen years. The sound river ice made every spring when it let go. The sea is loosening. But come out onto the ice. It isn't only the ice that's crying. Stand with me and hold up a lantern. Only a little while. There's too much sound to hear alone.",
 "Stand on the ice with Oscar, hold up a lantern, and listen to the thawing sea.",
 "It's the crew. Bound under the ice for fifteen years. With the king's gift taken back they're coming loose. But they don't leave. They go toward the ship. All of them. Toward the ghost ship. The captain is calling them. The king can't hold them now, so she holds them with her own hands. Without even weighing anchor. …She won't give up her law. Even if the king takes his gift back. I know that feeling. I lit a lantern for fifteen years. Let's go and meet her. Not to fight. To bring her word of the Morning Star."),
"story_3_30": ("The Ship Where No One Dies",
 "The ghost ship. Fifteen years in the ice's jaws. The sails haven't even rotted. Now it isn't the king's power holding it but the captain alone. Go aboard and read the ship's law and its roster. Know what kind of person the captain is before you meet her. We learned that in the land of the sun. Oscar goes with you. There's a line in that roster he needs to find.",
 "Find the ghost ship and examine the articles on the mast and the crew roster with Oscar.",
 "'No one dies on my ship.' Three hundred signed under that one line. And the roster has no column for death. The day they boarded, the day they left, and that's all. It's a ship you can't die on. Because the captain won't let go. Keeping her law. …Oscar read Elric's line and stood there a long time, I hear. He understood why fifteen years of trying to hate her never took. So the best kind of captain can become the most frightening kind. I was the opposite. A bad one. Which of the two loses more people, I couldn't say."),
"story_3_31": ("The Captain's Table",
 "Before we put the captain to rest we have to try speaking to her. With Helvar, and with that dragon, we only read. This time we have something to tell. The Morning Star. There's a table in the captain's cabin. She ate every evening at one table with her crew. One seat will be empty. The guest's seat. By sailors' law, when a guest sits at the table the captain must listen. Oscar told me. You can't sit down empty-handed. Take bread. Bread from the land.",
 "In the captain's cabin on the ghost ship, sit in the guest's seat with 3 bread and give the captain word of the Morning Star.",
 "What did she say? …'All hands ashore. That is for me to say. With my own mouth. And for that I must send my own three hundred ashore with my own mouth. But I cannot say those words. In thirty years I have never once said them.' And she drew her blade, you say. Not in anger. The captain can't break her law herself. Someone has to break it for her. If she duels and loses, then she hasn't abandoned it. She isn't one to throw a fight. And still she wants to lose."),
"story_3_32": ("Captain of the Frozen Sea",
 "Captain Cornelia. Open the Memory Gate. Her anchor has hung on the bell of the sunken citadel for fifteen years. Put her to rest outside a Gate and that bell rings whole. You saw the outpost. Inside the Gate. Her blade is poisoned. Bring antidote. She will see you as the death come for her crew. She isn't wrong. Go anyway. She wants to lose. She just doesn't know how to let someone win.",
 "Open the Memory Gate and put Captain Cornelia to rest.",
 "As she fell she called the names. Three hundred. Not one wrong. The last was Elric. Then she called thirty-two more. The Morning Star's. Down to Tobin. Only when she'd finished did she let go of her blade. And she said one thing. To her crew. The archivist took it down. Go and hear it. Then get to bed early. What's left is a frozen logbook. The anchor has come up. The bell did not ring."),
"story_3_33": ("The Logbook",
 "I wrote down what the captain said at the end. 'All hands ashore. Captain's orders.' The first time she'd said it in thirty years, they say. I thawed the logbook. Slowly, over three days. Thaw it fast and the ink runs. Let's read it together. After we write down the captain's name.",
 "Read the thawed logbook with Isabel the archivist.",
 "It's written. Cornelia. And the three hundred and the thirty-two. Roster and all. This stone was tucked between the pages. Carved with a storm. And the last page has a map. What the captain saw inside the storm. Seven dots shaped like eyes. I don't know what they are. I think we must ask the Elder. But listen. Some of these pages are stuck together. Glued, on purpose. There's a side the captain hid. It'll take me days to get them apart."),
"story_3_34": ("How to Go Ashore",
 "The captain said 'you may go ashore', did she. But men bound for fifteen years don't walk off at one word. They've forgotten how to leave. They're still circling the ship. Take my lantern. A sailor sees a light and takes it for the dock. Once at the shelter, once at the ship. First the ones who were walking out, then the ones who stayed aboard. That's the right order. Those who went ashore first, go first.",
 "Carry Oscar's lantern and gather the crew into it at the shelter and at the ghost ship.",
 "(Small lights waver inside the lantern.) …Hear that? The singing's stopped. Someone's laughing instead. Loud. 'Old man!' …It's Elric. He's in there. In the lantern. Fifteen years I lit this for him to see, and now he's come inside it."),
"story_3_35": ("A Heart That Can't Let Go",
 "The lantern's warm. First time in fifteen years. He's in here. Put it out and he goes. Where he's meant to go. Leave it lit and… he's here. Beside me. So this is what the captain did. Now I'm the captain. What do I do.",
 "Talk with Oscar the fisherman.",
 "…Then that's what I'll do. Thank you. Not for deciding for me. For standing here."),
"story_3_36": ("The Pages Glued Shut",
 "I got the glued pages apart. With steam. It's the side the captain hid on purpose. Not writing. A sketch. Under the floorboards of her cabin. She put something there. The ship should be quiet now. Please go and bring it out. I'd come, but Mia has a fever.",
 "In the captain's cabin on the ghost ship, examine what is hidden under the floorboards.",
 "An orb? Cracked? …That's the same as the one in our archive. The orb from my mother's things. And a drawing of the stand, you say. In purple ink. In mirror writing. And a note. 'Write down what you hear.' Signed with a gloved hand. …Two people. Two people gave the captain something. One sold her the route and one gave her the orb. That's what Haru meant about the grey coin having two different faces."),
"story_3_37": ("Mia's Mirror",
 "Mia's fever has broken. And she read that whole drawing in a mirror. She reads mirror writing better than I do. Perhaps because she's left-handed. She found something odd while reading. Hear it for yourself. After I heard it I stood in front of the archive's orb for a long time.",
 "Hear from Isabel the archivist what Mia the apprentice read.",
 "The stand we call the Rune Altar. The one we've used since we came back from putting the dragon to rest. Those five stones were never decoration. The drawing calls them 'five letters'. It doesn't say which. Whoever made it writes in the same hand as 'I rewrote this book' on the archive wall. The one who altered the Chronicle made a mirror that shows what's forgotten. Why? Why would the one who changed it try to hold on to what was lost? …I haven't told the Elder. I'll give him only the map. The orb can wait until we know a little more."),
"story_3_38": ("Casting the Net Again",
 "The sea's loose. Fish are back in the shallows. I cast a net for the first time in fifteen years. My hands remembered. Come and eat. Grilled cod. Done the way Elric liked it. Salt and nothing else. Let's eat it hot.",
 "Eat grilled cod with Oscar the fisherman and talk.",
 "How is it. …I can eat hot food again. Lorenzo cried. In the kitchen. Slamming pot lids so no one would hear. And one thing I want to ask you. The captain's logbook says the storm looked into the ship. One person at a time. I went inside it once myself, when I was young. We'll talk about it another time. When my heart has settled a little more."),
"story_3_39": ("The Dock Lamp",
 "Oscar is hanging a lamp on the dock. Not the one out on the ice, a new one. For any boat coming in. He'd like you to hang it. If he does it himself, he says, he'll only think of one person again. Meanwhile I'll take the captain's map to the Elder. The one with seven eyes marked on it. The Elder will know what it is. …That he'll know frightens me a little, these days.",
 "Hang a new lantern on the dock. (1 lantern)",
 "It's hung? I saw it too. You can see it from the archive window. And I saw the twentieth boat's name. 'Scarf.' …I shouldn't have laughed but I did. It's a good name. The Elder took the map and said nothing for a long while. Then, 'Send the expedition to me tomorrow.' His hands were shaking. Whether from the map or from the sea outside the window, I can't tell. His is the only room in the Order with a view of the sea, and he sits with his back to the window. I only just noticed. After fifteen years of going in and out."),
}
